# TI-59/PC-100A Program: Calendar Printer, by Richard Vanderburgh
#
# From 52-Notes. Part of the same calendar-program series as
# calendar-01-Weinberger.ti59, calendar-02-cargile.ti59,
# calendar-03-vanderburgh.ti59, and calendar-04-skillman.ti59.
#
# After printing V3N6p2 (calendar-04-skillman.ti59), the columnist
# received a calendar printer program from Panos Galidas (207) which
# uses Bill Skillman's doubled print-code method, but with a simpler way
# to handle all the special cases, printing a year in 5.5 minutes. This
# program is the columnist's own further revision of Galidas' approach --
# squeezed into 3 memory banks, using H5-8 (HIR, an undocumented keycode)
# in place of Op1-4 for the print-buffer left-right number shifting --
# printing a year in just under 5 minutes.
#
# The prestored data is unusually large, listed as steps 465-719 "for
# input convenience": the author suggests writing steps 000-326 and
# 465-719 under a 1 Op 17 partition, then storing the Reg 62-78 constants
# under an 8 Op 17 partition (to avoid data shifts from the differing
# register layout), then recording the magnetic card banks under a 6 Op
# 17 partition. The effective partition during actual execution is
# 327.78 -- i.e. steps 000-326 are the real (executable) program, and
# 465-719 are pure data smuggled into program memory, not real code.
# This preset uses PARTITION: 319.79 (320 steps / 80 data registers),
# matching the "1 Op 17" partition the article uses for the listed steps.
#
# User Instructions: same as calendar-03-vanderburgh.ti59 (V3N6p2), except
# there is no CROM module test in this revision.
# 1. Key start year, press A.
# 2. Key start month, press R/S.
#    For 1 Month: Press B; for balance of year, press C; for specified
#    interval, key end year, press D.
#
# Transcribed from a compact magazine/manual listing (OCR'd), same
# shorthand dialect as the rest of this series (see the
# calc-u-59-statefiles skill's "52-Notes" section). Notes specific to
# this listing:
#   - "H<n>" is HIR (an undocumented keycode, 82) with operand n, e.g.
#     "H7" = HIR 07 (82, 07). Used here in place of the more common
#     Op1-4 print-buffer-loading instructions, per the intro text.
#   - "CLR'" (an apostrophe after a mnemonic, here and elsewhere) is 2nd
#     CLR -- keycode 20 -- distinct from the bare "CLR" key (keycode 25)
#     used elsewhere in this same listing; there is no printed 2nd-row
#     label above the CLR key on the physical keyboard, but the keycode
#     is real and distinct.
#   - "/" is divide (55); "+/-" is change-sign (94).
#   - Steps 465-719 are NOT valid program code -- per the article, this
#     range is prestored *data* (more print-buffer constants than fit in
#     the "Prestored Data" section below) that the author had you key in
#     as if it were a program listing purely so the magazine could print
#     it compactly. Accordingly this section decodes as a flat sequence
#     of single-byte values (never multi-step instructions), verified
#     against every one of its thirteen declared line-address boundaries
#     (465, 485, 506, 525, 544, 564, 584, 603, 622, 640, 663, 687, 710)
#     and the final total (720 bytes, i.e. steps 465-719 inclusive,
#     matching the article's own claim exactly):
#       - "0"/"O" = 0; "00" = two consecutive zero bytes (not one).
#       - "E'" = 10; "Ind" = 40 (IND); "Rad"/"Deg" = 70/60 (2nd Rad/Deg);
#         "Abs" = 50 (2nd |x|); "tan" = 30 (2nd 1/x, whose 2nd function
#         is tan); "CLR"/"CLR'" = 25/20 as above.
#       - "p31"/"p21" (lowercase in the source, unlike the real "P"rogram
#         key) is a single byte holding the literal value 31/21 -- not
#         "Pgm 31"/"Pgm 21" (which would need 2 bytes: 36 then the
#         operand). The single-byte reading is what the checksum requires
#         throughout (a 2-byte reading overshoots every line's declared
#         length); since this whole range is acknowledged non-code data
#         anyway, "p" is most likely a marginal annotation rather than a
#         real keystroke, not unlike the "O" = 0 substitution seen
#         elsewhere in this series.
# Program length (steps 000-326) reconstructed step-by-step and
# cross-checked against the step numbers printed at the start of every
# listing line (000, 029, 056, 086, 112, 139, 169, 198, 227, 258, 289,
# 320) -- every line's decoded length matches its declared next-line
# boundary exactly, ending at step 326 as the article states.
# Uses Master Library program 20 (day-of-week / days-in-month helpers, via
# SBR 086), hence SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML below.

MODEL: TI-59
PARTITION: 319.79
SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML
PRINTER: on

CUECARD:
Template: MagnetCard
Title: Calendar Printer
Banks: 1,3
A: START YEAR
B: 1 MONTH
C: TO YR END
D: END YEAR

